#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d6988b-3220-578f-ba1c-cd86b9e3dbf9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and the Radius"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccfafb5a-9d68-5430-9678-7f8ae2289013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccfafb5a-9d68-5430-9678-7f8ae2289013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355434} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa87df0-384d-5845-b3ae-a2f670937ca2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aa5ec7e-35c7-5308-9c01-0b177011d114" :question/math-academy-id "q-167932" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65a946d0-79ba-5e8b-b9f7-711165ff03b3" :knowledge-point/title "Equations Where the Perfect Square Is Not Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdc1ae0-9e9c-595a-9116-7f50c4059e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cdc1ae0-9e9c-595a-9116-7f50c4059e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355437} :answer-field/id #uuid "77cd618d-acc5-5bad-b798-3f9c28b43a83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ef8d58-b9fb-5237-9e30-b23320c77477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ef8d58-b9fb-5237-9e30-b23320c77477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355439} :answer-field/id #uuid "f70ec80e-aee3-5a66-8875-7256c144673a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb21d9e6-1c6c-57bb-a085-f049842e2ff8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167933" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9-(x-3)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^{2}} \\\\ |y| & =\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-6x+9)} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n* the equation with the positive square root, $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the upper semicircle, and \n\n* the equation with the negative square root, $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the lower semicircle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee633017-5c17-575f-bdab-5f12b7319355" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral Given the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc964dfa-8698-5dd3-baf1-b3b908ce2269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc964dfa-8698-5dd3-baf1-b3b908ce2269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355442} :answer-field/id #uuid "64f4e816-a967-506c-9f77-f8a829a4caa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efa63c4c-aff1-5662-a025-40591a1f5ee0" :question/math-academy-id "q-167934" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle  and  $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle, the area $A$ of the circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})-(-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}2\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8babcffe-a60c-56b8-aa30-e9e2322a9060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355445} {:answer/id #uuid "073eee2b-5061-55c2-a299-9964960b5045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8babcffe-a60c-56b8-aa30-e9e2322a9060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355445} :answer-field/id #uuid "1caacf60-e80b-5931-8f71-8e28b6c628ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d48b736-e08a-5fcb-89ef-4baef85fd7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167935" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${f}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle and ${f}_{2}(x)=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle with $a=0$ and $b=6.$ \n\nFirst, we find the derivative of ${f}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}_{1}^{′}(x)$ into the arc length formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{1} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}_{1}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(3-x)^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{\\frac{6x-{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9-6x+{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the same manner, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L={L}_{1}+{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Areas Bounded by Other Polar Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97479b02-9217-5d9c-bda1-7f4b0ab277bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(\\cos(\\theta))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97479b02-9217-5d9c-bda1-7f4b0ab277bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(\\cos(\\theta))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355449} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb100120-e6fc-5d5f-97dd-2d01d6c34520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2bb3d235-8608-5dc5-b1e6-8b31b43eeb0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167937" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}36{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}18{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd0b4da9-1a0f-5a46-aff1-3ce5c0cebb3b" :knowledge-point/title "Creating an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78690cc0-4536-5b23-a5dd-1fcabc67ebfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78690cc0-4536-5b23-a5dd-1fcabc67ebfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355452} :answer-field/id #uuid "adbe6a1e-f97a-5a05-bd1a-08525fe36ed7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff226c50-e59e-5c6f-b42e-e5a324df1531" :question/math-academy-id "q-167938" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $18(\\cos(\\theta))^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55fab3ac-cad3-54bd-b07b-9159554b634c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9fd2b99-3667-54f4-8886-9aa4d105277c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9fd2b99-3667-54f4-8886-9aa4d105277c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355455} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6a6e29-11a5-5f97-b543-9b95e8daac1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9b9284f-9dca-50da-8298-0c30e47654fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-167939" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $18(\\cos(\\theta))^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nConstruct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe average distance from the origin to a point on the circumference of the circle is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Since $r(\\theta )$ represents the distance of each point on the circle from the origin, the average distance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6-(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
